    "q-83480": {
      "dom_id": "step-q83480",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Find an eigenvector of $A=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =3.$",
      "worked_solution": "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\lambda =3.$ So, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 7 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 6\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the eigenvectors corresponding to this eigenvalue, we have to solve the corresponding homogeneous system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -9{x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}+5{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we row-reduce the augmented matrix $M$ to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 4 & 5 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-\\frac{9}{5}){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{2}{5} & -\\frac{2}{5} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there are two pivot columns (the $1$st and the $2$nd). Thus, ${x}_{3}$ is a free variable. From the second equation, we obtain ${x}_{2}={x}_{3}.$ Substituting this in the first equation, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{2}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}(2{x}_{3}+3{x}_{3}) \\\\  & ={x}_{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting, for instance, ${x}_{3}=1,$ we get the eigenvector\n$v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
